    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Thanks. To be more specific, I'm seeing cases where the morphology is just... strange. Like, some of these lesions have this very irregular, bumpy texture that actually looks almost like something is living under the skin, or there are these small, dark, knobby protrusions that don't follow any known dermatological pattern. When the physician's notes describe the texture as \"gnarled\" or \"irregularly pebbled,\" how should that influence the choice between a code for a benign neoplasm versus a more complex descriptor for an unspecified skin disorder? I'm trying to avoid denials based on \"lack of specificity\" when the lesion itself is visually very odd.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "\"Gnarled\" or \"irregularly pebbled\" textures strongly suggest a need to explore codes related to surface changes or abnormal growths rather than relying on simple benign code descriptions. Before coding an unspecified disorder, try to link the morphology to the clinical suspicion, perhaps using codes for uncertain or indeterminate lesions. If all else fails, the documentation must explicitly support why a specific diagnosis cannot be made over a broad, yet appropriate, category of skin neoplasm or disorder.",
      "source": "target",
