    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense, but we're still seeing a lot of pushback from auditors on these specific \"atypical\" cases. There's a particular cluster of cases where the lesions are noted as having a coarse, almost subterranean or cave-like texture, and the pigmentation is described as being very mottled and dark, almost like dirt or silt. It feels like we're hitting a wall with the standard ICD-10 hierarchy because these don't look like typical human dermatological presentations. Is there any protocol for when a lesion's physical appearance is so completely outside the norm that it seems to defy standard clinical categories?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "When a physical description defies standard dermatological categorization, the focus must shift to capturing the *reason* for the visit over the lesion's exact morphology. If the appearance suggests an unusual or chronic process, codes for generalized skin conditions or those related to an underlying systemic issue might be more appropriate than a specific lesion code. Ultimately, auditors require evidence that the provider considered differential diagnoses; ensure the chart reflects this broad clinical evaluation.",
      "source": "target",
